<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title><%= title %></title>
    <meta name="description" content="<%= description %>" />
    <link rel="canonical" href="<%= canonicalUrl %>" />
    <%- include('partials/seo-social', { ogTitle: title, ogDescription: description, ogUrl: canonicalUrl }) %>
    <%- include('partials/favicon-links') %>
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Space+Grotesk:wght@400;500;600;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/home.css?v=<%= assetVersion %>" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/webmcp-directory.css?v=<%= assetVersion %>" />
  </head>
  <body class="registry-page webmcp-page">
    <%- include('partials/home-header') %>
    <main class="dir-shell webmcp-prose" style="padding:2rem 1rem;">
      <p class="webmcp-note"><a href="/webmcp">← WebMCP Directory</a></p>
      <h1>About the Influzer WebMCP Directory</h1>
      <p><strong>WebMCP</strong> lets websites expose structured tools to AI agents in the browser. This directory helps you discover those websites, inspect tool schemas, and understand capability boundaries.</p>
      <h2>WebMCP is not an MCP server</h2>
      <p><a href="/mcp">MCP servers</a> are durable external integrations your AI client connects to (GitHub, databases, browsers). <strong>WebMCP tools</strong> are page-scoped capabilities declared by a website itself. Influzer catalogs both — separately — so the distinction stays clear.</p>
      <h2>Try native WebMCP in Chrome</h2>
      <p>Use Chrome Canary or Beta (version 146+). Open <code>chrome://flags/#enable-webmcp-testing</code>, set <strong>WebMCP for testing</strong> to Enabled, relaunch, then open the <a href="/webmcp/demo">live demo</a>. Mode should say Native. Full steps (Chrome flag + Edge origin trial): <a href="/webmcp/setup">Set up and launch WebMCP</a>.</p>
      <h2>What “verified” means</h2>
      <p>Verified means Influzer observed the implementation (or confirmed it from a primary source). Seed imports from public directories start as <em>unverified</em> until an Influzer scan or review confirms them. Verified is not a safety endorsement.</p>
      <h2>Answer / Act / Transact</h2>
      <p>Tool kinds are a trust taxonomy: answer (read/info), act (mutate state), transact (money or irreversible outcomes). They are classifications, not permissions.</p>
      <h2>Data sources</h2>
      <p>Influzer owns the normalized snapshot. Public directories may seed discovery; provenance is shown on site pages. If an upstream feed is unavailable, the last good Influzer snapshot remains live.</p>
      <p><a class="webmcp-btn webmcp-btn--primary" href="/webmcp">Browse the directory</a>
      <a class="webmcp-btn webmcp-btn--ghost" href="/webmcp/demo">Try Influzer’s live WebMCP</a>
      <a class="webmcp-btn webmcp-btn--ghost" href="/webmcp/setup">Setup &amp; launch guide</a>
      <a class="webmcp-btn webmcp-btn--ghost" href="/insights/what-is-webmcp-agents-navigate-websites">Read the explainer</a></p>
    </main>
    <%- include('partials/registry-footer') %>
    <script src="/js/site-auth.js?v=<%= assetVersion %>" defer></script>
    <%- include('partials/influzer-webmcp-script') %>
  </body>
</html>
